{{also|Brethren}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|brethern}} {{q|[[obsolete]]}}

===Etymology===
From Early Modern English {{m|en||brethren}}, plural of [[brother]], from {{inh|en|enm|brethren}}, from {{der|en|enm|brethere}}, {{m|enm|brether}} + {{m|enm|-en|pos=plural ending}}. Ultimately from {{der|en|ang|brōþor}}, {{m|ang|brōþru|t=brothers, brethren}}, influenced by {{cog|ang|brēþer}}, dative singular of {{m|ang|brōþor|t=brother}}. Equivalent to {{af|en|brother|-en|g2=p}}. Compare {{cog|de|Brüder||brothers, brethren}}. More at {{l|en|brother}}. The vowel change (from ''o'' to ''e'') is called [[w:Germanic umlaut|umlaut]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbɹɛðɹən/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-brethren.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{head|en|noun form}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} {{plural of|en|brother}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} The body of members, especially of a fraternal, religious or military order.

====Usage notes====
The plural {{m|en|brethren}} is generally used for members of an organization, especially a religious body, whereas the plural {{m|en|brothers}} is used in the familial sense as well as for larger groups.

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|brethren-in-law}}

